## Schema

Each row is one polling observation for one JVM process (`pid`) during a CI run (`run_id`).

| Column | Type | Unit / Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `run_id` | `string` | Anonymized 16-char salted SHA-256 hash identifying one CI build job |
| `sample_timestamp` | `timestamp` | UTC observation timestamp |
| `elapsed_time` | `int64` | Seconds elapsed since monitoring started for the run |
| `pid` | `string` | OS process ID within the CI runner (unique only within a `run_id`) |
| `name` | `string` | JVM process type: `GradleDaemon`, `KotlinCompileDaemon`, or `GradleWorkerMain` |
| `heap_used` | `int64` | JVM heap used (MB) |
| `heap_cap` | `int64` | Committed JVM heap capacity (MB) |
| `rss` | `int64` | Resident Set Size (MB) |
| `gc_time` | `int64` | Cumulative garbage collection time (ms) |
| `jit_compiled_methods` | `int64` | Cumulative JIT compiled methods (`jstat -compiler`) |
| `jit_failed_compilations` | `int64` | Cumulative failed JIT compilations |
| `jit_invalidated_compilations` | `int64` | Cumulative invalidated JIT compilations |
| `jit_compilation_time_ms` | `int64` | Cumulative compiler-thread elapsed time (ms; not process CPU time) |
| `classes_loaded` | `int64` | Cumulative loaded classes (`jstat -class`) |
| `classes_unloaded` | `int64` | Cumulative unloaded classes |
| `class_load_time_ms` | `int64` | Cumulative class-loading time (ms) |
| `run_finished_at` | `timestamp` | UTC timestamp when the monitored CI job finished |

## Cleaning & Anonymization

Published reproducibly via [`cdsap/build-process-dataset`](https://github.com/cdsap/build-process-dataset):

- `run_id` is replaced with a deterministic 16-character salted SHA-256 hex digest.
- No source code, repository names, task graphs, or JVM launch flags (`vm_flags`) are included.
- Deduplicated on `(run_id, pid, sample_timestamp)`.
- Filtered to rows with complete non-null JIT (`jstat -compiler`) and class-loading (`jstat -class`) counters, non-negative metrics, positive `rss`, and runs with at least 3 samples.

## Usage

```python
from datasets import load_dataset

ds = load_dataset("inakivillar/build-process-dataset", split="train")
print(ds)
```

### Leak-Free Chronological Split (Recommended for Forecasting)

Because rows within a single `run_id` are sequential 5-second observations, **always split by `run_id`** (never randomly by row). For forecasting benchmarks, split chronologically by `run_finished_at`:

```python
import pandas as pd
from datasets import load_dataset

df = load_dataset("inakivillar/build-process-dataset", split="train").to_pandas()
runs = df.groupby("run_id")["run_finished_at"].max().sort_values()

n = len(runs)
train_ids = set(runs.index[: int(n * 0.70)])
val_ids = set(runs.index[int(n * 0.70) : int(n * 0.85)])
test_ids = set(runs.index[int(n * 0.85) :])

train_df = df[df["run_id"].isin(train_ids)]
val_df = df[df["run_id"].isin(val_ids)]
test_df = df[df["run_id"].isin(test_ids)]
```

